# Line endings are part of this repository's contract, not a local preference.
#
# Git for Windows checks out with core.autocrlf=true by default, and until this file existed a
# Windows clone got CRLF for every tracked file. That is not cosmetic here, because the engine
# generates its harness artifacts by string surgery over these very files:
#
#   - the Codex and OpenCode skill writers locate the end of a page's YAML frontmatter before
#     stamping their ownership marker. A CRLF checkout made that search miss, the marker landed
#     BEFORE the opening `---`, and Codex then refused to load ALL FIVE skills with "missing YAML
#     frontmatter delimited by ---" — while `llmwiki doctor` reported them present, because it
#     checked that the files existed and not that they parsed.
#   - `skill/*.md` also had `name:` injected via /^---\n/, which a CRLF file never matches.
#   - the shell scripts (setup.sh, daemon/*.sh, hooks/*.sh) have the same exposure with a wider
#     blast radius. Git Bash tolerates a trailing CR today; other shells on the same machine
#     (WSL, MSYS2, busybox) do not, and "it worked on my bash" is not a contract.
#
# The engine is also hardened against CRLF input, because this file cannot fix clones that already
# exist — it only governs checkouts from here on. Both are needed: one keeps the bytes right, the
# other survives bytes that are not.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Byte-exact regardless of platform.
*.png binary
*.zst binary
*.db binary

# Transcript fixtures are read by BYTE offset (see src/engine/extract.ts): a platform that
# rewrote their line endings would move every watermark in the suite.
tests/fixtures/**/*.jsonl -text
tests/*.jsonl -text
